    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe logic is placed correctly \u2014 `toAgeBand` sits alongside the other derived-field helpers (`toCheckDigit`, `toIdentityKey`, `toMembershipLevel`, `toContactPreference`, `toLocality`), it's a cohesive single-purpose default method wired in via the same `@Mapping(... expression = \"java(...)\")` pattern, and it introduces no duplication or catch-all bloat. The `Owner` field/accessor and schema additions are the natural, symmetric places for the new attribute. That part is idiomatic.\n\nThe one structural issue is stylistic inconsistency with the rest of this file:\n\n- `toAgeBand` uses fully-qualified class names inline everywhere (`java.time.LocalDate`, `java.time.Period`, and repeatedly `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto.AgeBandEnum`), whereas every other method in `OwnerMapper` relies on top-of-file imports (`Map`, `Integer`, `String`, etc.). This makes the new method a visible outlier and hurts readability \u2014 the return type and the three enum references are especially noisy.\n  - `OwnerDto` is *already imported* (line 8), so the return type and enum constants should be written as `OwnerDto.AgeBandEnum` / `OwnerDto.AgeBandEnum.MINOR`, etc.\n  - Add imports for `java.time.LocalDate` and `java.time.Period` (the file already has a `java.time`-free import block with `java.util.*`; add these next to them) and drop the `java.time.` prefixes in the body.\n\nMaking these use the same import-based style as the surrounding helpers is what a maintainer of this file would expect; nothing else about the change needs to move.",
